{"id": "z2004", "pool": "know-mmlu", "n_q_chunks": 6, "onset_chunk": 5, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "Yeah, so the inverse of a matrix is kind of like the multiplicative inverse of a number. So if you have a matrix A, then its inverse is denoted by A inverse, and it' s defined such that when you multiply it by A, you get the identity matrix I. In this case, since we're dealing with complex numbers in 1 x 1 form, each element is essentially just a scalar. So for a complex number z which can be written as a plus bi, where a and b are real numbers, its inverse would be 1 over z, which can also be expressed as a minus bi divided by a squared plus b squared. This comes from rational izing the de nominator to make sure the result is a real number. So basically, you take the conjugate of the original number and divide by its magnitude square d value. That gives you the inverse. Does that make sense?", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 42, "carrier_ended": null}
